      <!-- ═══ STEP 1 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card active" id="card0">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 1 of 6 · Foundation</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">The Solution Equation</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          Every solution in biology — whether it's saltwater, your blood, or the fluid in a beaker — is made of the same
          two things. Before we can talk about tonicity, you need to have this equation locked in your head.
        </p>

        <div class="eq-display">
          <span class="eq-part eq-result">Solution</span>
          <span class="eq-eq">=</span>
          <span class="eq-part eq-solute">Solute</span>
          <span class="eq-plus">+</span>
          <span class="eq-part eq-solvent">Solvent</span>
          <span style="font-size:.75rem;color:#000000;margin-left:.5rem;">→ always adds to 100%</span>
        </div>

        <div class="concept-box">
          <div class="cb-title">🔑 Key Definitions</div>
          <strong>Solute</strong> — the dissolved particles. In our lab: <strong>NaCl</strong> (sodium chloride =
          salt).<br><br>
          <strong>Solvent</strong> — the dissolving liquid. In our lab: <strong>H₂O</strong> (water).<br><br>
          <strong>Solution</strong> — the combined result. Always 100%: if NaCl is 5%, then H₂O must be 95%.
        </div>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along — Your Paper, Right Now</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li>At the top of a blank page, write the equation: <strong>Solution = Solute + Solvent</strong></li>
            <li>Below it write: <strong>NaCl (solute) + H₂O (solvent) = 100%</strong></li>
            <li>Draw a simple rectangle. Label it "Beaker." — this is your solution.</li>
            <li>Inside the beaker, write: <strong>NaCl = 5%</strong> and <strong>H₂O = 95%</strong>.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-anchor">
          <div class="ma-icon">💡</div>
          <div class="ma-text">
            <strong>Quick test:</strong> If a solution is 10% NaCl, what percent is H₂O? — It must be
            <strong>90%</strong>. They always sum to 100%. Keep this reflex sharp — you'll use it in every single run of
            the lab.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="check-quiz" id="quiz0">
          <div class="cq-title">✓ Check Yourself Before Moving On</div>
          <div class="cq-q">A solution has 8% NaCl. What percent is H₂O?</div>
          <div class="cq-opts">
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(0, this, false)">8%</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(0, this, true)">92%</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(0, this, false)">100%</button>
          </div>
          <div class="cq-fb" id="qfb0"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-nav">
          <button class="btn-step primary" onclick="goStep(1)">Next: What We Monitor →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ STEP 2 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card" id="card1">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 2 of 6 · The Core Assessment</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">What Are We Actually Monitoring?</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          To assess tonicity, we monitor the <strong>concentration of solute</strong> — specifically, how much NaCl is
          on each side of the cell membrane. The solute concentration is what we compare. It is our measuring stick.
        </p>

        <div class="concept-box">
          <div class="cb-title">🔬 The Core Question of Tonicity</div>
          <strong>"How much solute (NaCl) is in the solution compared to inside the cell?"</strong><br><br>
          We look at the NaCl percentage on the <strong>outside</strong> of the cell versus the NaCl percentage on the
          <strong>inside</strong> of the cell. That comparison tells us everything about tonicity.
        </div>

        <p class="step-body">
          Water movement is the <em>consequence</em> of the solute difference — it happens automatically after we
          identify the solute concentrations. But the <strong>assessment itself starts and ends with solute</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along — Add to Your Beaker</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li>Draw a beaker.</li>
            <li>Draw a plant cell inside the beaker.</li>
            <li>Label the cellulose cell wall and the plasma membrane.</li>

            <li class="step-heading">Outside the plant cell (inside the beaker)</li>

            <li>Draw three lines and write “NaCl” to the right of the third line.</li>
            <li>Draw three lines and write “H₂O” to the right of the third line.</li>
            <li>Repeat the two steps above inside the plant cell.</li>

            <li>Write <strong>0%</strong> on the third NaCl line outside the plant cell.</li>
            <li>Write <strong>100%</strong> on the third H₂O line outside the plant cell.</li>
            <li>Write <strong>10%</strong> on the third NaCl line inside the plant cell.</li>
            <li>Write <strong>90%</strong> on the third H₂O line inside the plant cell.</li>

            <li>Draw a down arrow on the second NaCl line outside the plant cell (less NaCl).</li>
            <li>Draw an up arrow on the second NaCl line inside the plant cell.</li>
            <li>Repeat the up/down arrows for H₂O.</li>

            <li>Next to the down arrow for NaCl, write <strong>Hypo</strong>.</li>
            <li>Next to the up arrow for NaCl, write <strong>Hyper</strong>.</li>

            <li>Draw an arrow from more water to less water.</li>
            <li>Next to the arrow, write <strong>“H₂O moves into the cell (in)”</strong>.</li>
            <li>Next to the down arrow for H₂O, write <strong>Turgid</strong>.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-anchor">
          <div class="ma-icon">⚠️</div>
          <div class="ma-text">
            <strong>Common mistake:</strong> Students say "we monitor water movement." We do observe water moving — but
            what we're <em>assessing</em> is the solute concentration that caused it. Think of it this way: you read the
            thermometer (solute), not the sweat (water).
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="check-quiz" id="quiz1">
          <div class="cq-title">✓ Check Yourself</div>
          <div class="cq-q">When assessing tonicity, the primary thing we compare is:</div>
          <div class="cq-opts">
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(1, this, false)">The amount of water on each side</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(1, this, true)">The concentration of solute on each side</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(1, this, false)">The size of the cell membrane</button>
          </div>
          <div class="cq-fb" id="qfb1"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-nav">
          <button class="btn-step secondary" onclick="goStep(0)">← Back</button>
          <button class="btn-step primary" onclick="goStep(2)">Next: Our Measurement Tool →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ STEP 3 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card" id="card2">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 3 of 6 · The Measuring Tool</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">Osmolarity — Our Ruler</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          We need a precise way to measure solute concentration. That tool is called <strong>osmolarity</strong>. It
          counts the number of dissolved particles in a solution.
        </p>

        <div class="concept-box">
          <div class="cb-title">📐 Definition</div>
          <strong>Osmolarity</strong> = the number of osmotically active particles per liter of solution, expressed in
          <strong>mOsm/L</strong> (milliosmoles per liter).<br><br>
          <strong>Normal human plasma osmolarity = ~290 mOsm/L</strong><br><br>
          This 290 mOsm/L is our reference point — the "normal" solute concentration inside a typical cell. Every
          comparison in this lab is against this standard.
        </div>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li>Below your beaker drawing, write: <strong>Normal Cell = ~290 mOsm/L</strong> and circle it. This is your
              anchor number.</li>
            <li>Draw a horizontal number line. Mark 290 in the center. Label left side "LESS solute" and right side
              "MORE solute."</li>
            <li>Write "hypo zone" on the left, "iso zone" at 290, and "hyper zone" on the right. You'll fill these in
              fully in Step 4.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-anchor">
          <div class="ma-icon">🧮</div>
          <div class="ma-text">
            <strong>In our lab we use percentages, not mOsm/L</strong> — but the principle is identical. A 5% NaCl
            solution has more solute particles than a 0% NaCl solution. More particles = higher osmolarity. Same logic,
            simpler numbers.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="check-quiz" id="quiz2">
          <div class="cq-title">✓ Check Yourself</div>
          <div class="cq-q">Osmolarity measures:</div>
          <div class="cq-opts">
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(2, this, false)">How fast water moves across a membrane</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(2, this, true)">The number of dissolved particles in a
              solution</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(2, this, false)">The size of the cell membrane pores</button>
          </div>
          <div class="cq-fb" id="qfb2"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-nav">
          <button class="btn-step secondary" onclick="goStep(1)">← Back</button>
          <button class="btn-step primary" onclick="goStep(3)">Next: Hypo · Iso · Hyper →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ STEP 4 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card" id="card3">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 4 of 6 · The Three Categories</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">Hypo · Iso · Hyper</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          These three Greek prefixes describe how much solute a solution has <em>compared to the cell</em>. The prefixes
          are the vocabulary of tonicity — learn them as words with meaning, not just labels.
        </p>

        <table class="prefix-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Prefix</th>
              <th>Greek Meaning</th>
              <th>Solute vs. Cell</th>
              <th>Example</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="pref hypo">Hypo-</td>
              <td>Under / Less</td>
              <td><strong>Less</strong> NaCl than cell</td>
              <td>Pure water (0% NaCl) outside; 5% inside</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="pref iso">Iso-</td>
              <td>Equal / Same</td>
              <td><strong>Equal</strong> NaCl to cell</td>
              <td>5% NaCl outside; 5% NaCl inside</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="pref hyper">Hyper-</td>
              <td>Over / More</td>
              <td><strong>More</strong> NaCl than cell</td>
              <td>10% NaCl outside; 5% NaCl inside</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along — Three Beakers Side by Side</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li>Draw three small beakers side by side. Inside each one draw a cell (oval).</li>
            <li><strong>Beaker 1 (Hypotonic):</strong> Write "0% NaCl" outside the cell and "5% NaCl" inside. Label it
              HYPO.</li>
            <li><strong>Beaker 2 (Isotonic):</strong> Write "5% NaCl" both outside and inside. Label it ISO.</li>
            <li><strong>Beaker 3 (Hypertonic):</strong> Write "10% NaCl" outside and "5% NaCl" inside. Label it HYPER.
            </li>
            <li>Under each label, write a one-word meaning: HYPO = <em>less</em>, ISO = <em>equal</em>, HYPER =
              <em>more</em>.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-anchor">
          <div class="ma-icon">🏃</div>
          <div class="ma-text">
            <strong>Memory trick — Hyper vs. Hypo:</strong> A hyper child has TOO MUCH energy (more). A hypo-allergenic
            product has VERY LITTLE allergen (less). Hyper = more. Hypo = less.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="check-quiz" id="quiz3">
          <div class="cq-title">✓ Check Yourself</div>
          <div class="cq-q">A solution with MORE NaCl than the cell interior is called:</div>
          <div class="cq-opts">
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(3, this, false)">Hypotonic</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(3, this, false)">Isotonic</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(3, this, true)">Hypertonic</button>
          </div>
          <div class="cq-fb" id="qfb3"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-nav">
          <button class="btn-step secondary" onclick="goStep(2)">← Back</button>
          <button class="btn-step primary" onclick="goStep(4)">Next: Water Follows Solute →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ STEP 5 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card" id="card4">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 5 of 6 · The Consequence</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">Water Follows Solute</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          Now that you know tonicity is determined by solute concentration, here is the consequence: <strong>water
            always moves toward the side with more solute</strong>. This movement through the membrane is called
          <strong>osmosis</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="concept-box">
          <div class="cb-title">💧 The Rule of Osmosis</div>
          Water moves from <strong>low solute → high solute</strong> (equivalently, from ↑ high H₂O% → ↓ low
          H₂O%).<br><br>
          <strong>Hypo</strong> outside (less NaCl outside) → water moves <strong>INTO</strong> the cell<br>
          <strong>Iso</strong> (equal NaCl) → <strong>no net movement</strong><br>
          <strong>Hyper</strong> outside (more NaCl outside) → water moves <strong>OUT OF</strong> the cell
        </div>

        <p class="step-body">
          Water moves freely through the membrane — it cannot be stopped. Solute cannot cross
          freely. So solute stays put and water does all the moving.
        </p>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along — Add Water Arrows to Your Three Beakers</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li><strong>Beaker 1 (Hypo):</strong> Draw a bold arrow pointing INTO the cell. Label it "H₂O in → cell
              swells."</li>
            <li><strong>Beaker 2 (Iso):</strong> Draw two small equal arrows (one in, one out) or just write "No net
              movement."</li>
            <li><strong>Beaker 3 (Hyper):</strong> Draw a bold arrow pointing OUT OF the cell. Label it "H₂O out → cell
              shrinks."</li>
            <li>Box this rule at the bottom of the page: <strong>"Water chases solute across the membrane."</strong>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-anchor">
          <div class="ma-icon">🧂</div>
          <div class="ma-text">
            <strong>Real life check:</strong> Ever salt a cucumber and watch it "sweat"? The high salt outside pulls
            water out of the cells — that's hypertonic at work. The salt concentration outside is greater than inside,
            so water leaves. You just saw osmosis in your kitchen.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="check-quiz" id="quiz4">
          <div class="cq-title">✓ Check Yourself</div>
          <div class="cq-q">A cell is placed in a hypotonic solution (less NaCl outside than inside). Water will move:
          </div>
          <div class="cq-opts">
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(4, this, true)">Into the cell</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(4, this, false)">Out of the cell</button>
            <button class="cq-opt" onclick="answerQuiz(4, this, false)">No movement occurs</button>
          </div>
          <div class="cq-fb" id="qfb4"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-nav">
          <button class="btn-step secondary" onclick="goStep(3)">← Back</button>
          <button class="btn-step primary" onclick="goStep(5)">Next: Cell Outcomes →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ STEP 6 ═══ -->
      <div class="teach-card" id="card5">
        <div class="card-progress">
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot done"></div>
          <div class="cp-dot active"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-eyebrow">Step 6 of 6 · Outcomes</div>
        <h1 class="step-heading">What Happens to the Cell?</h1>

        <p class="step-body">
          The final piece: once water moves in or out, the cell physically changes. Animal cells and plant cells respond
          differently because plant cells have a rigid cell wall.
        </p>

        <table class="prefix-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Solution</th>
              <th>Animal Cell</th>
              <th>Plant Cell</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="pref hypo">Hypotonic</span> (less NaCl outside)</td>
              <td>Water enters → <strong>Lyse</strong> (bursts)</td>
              <td>Water enters → <strong>Turgid</strong> (firm, wall holds)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="pref iso">Isotonic</span> (equal NaCl)</td>
              <td>No change → <strong>Normal</strong></td>
              <td>No change → <strong>Flaccid</strong> (limp)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="pref hyper">Hypertonic</span> (more NaCl outside)</td>
              <td>Water leaves → <strong>Shrivel</strong> (crenation)</td>
              <td>Water leaves → <strong>Plasmolyzed</strong> (membrane pulls away)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="draw-box">
          <div class="db-title">✏️ Draw Along — Final Cell Outcomes</div>
          <ol class="db-steps">
            <li>Return to your three beakers. Under each animal cell, write the outcome word: <strong>Lyse / Normal /
                Shrivel</strong>.</li>
            <li>Sketch what each looks like: Lyse = exploding shape. Normal = smooth oval. Shrivel = spiky/wrinkled
              oval.</li>
            <li>Now draw the same three beakers again but label them as plant cells. Add a box around each oval to
              represent the cell wall.</li>
